---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide class="cover-internal">
  <h1>The near future</h1>

  <PNotes>
    <p>This section is honest speculation, not "use this Monday".</p>
    <p>
      The point is not production readiness. The point is direction: CSS is
      moving logic closer to values and components - conditions, functions,
      tokens read straight from HTML.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three things: if(), @function, typed attr(). Some shipping in one or two
      engines - always check support before building on them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Callback: style queries were in this section at CSS Day in June. Since May
      they're Baseline. That's how fast this moves.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide class="cover-internal-sub-section">
  <h2>if()</h2>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Be precise: this is the if() function, inline in a value - not a block
      @if.
    </p>
    <p>
      It builds on what we just saw: the style() condition is the same one style
      queries use.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="main-gradient">Inline conditional values</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      🔀 <span>Pick a value </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">inline</span>
      <span>, inside one declaration</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      📏 <span>One </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">property value</span>
      <span> at a time - not whole blocks</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Frame the difference vs @container: if() sets a single value; @container
      applies a whole rule set.
    </p>
    <p>
      They are complementary, not rivals: one is inline value selection, the
      other is conditional rule application.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h5 style="margin-block-end: 1em" class="center">
    <span class="main-gradient">if() - a value, conditionally</span> 🔀
  </h5>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="if-function.css">
    <code style="font-size: 0.7em" is:raw class="language-scss">
    section {
        --tone: loud;
    }

    article {
        background: if(
            style(--tone: loud): oklch(0.7 0.2 250);
            else: oklch(0.95 0.02 250)
        );
    }</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Read it as: if the container exposes --tone: loud, use the loud
      background; otherwise use the muted background.
    </p>
    <p>
      Same idea as the style query slides, but now the conditional choice
      happens inside one property value.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide class="cover-internal-sub-section">
  <h2>
    <span style="font-family: Arial;">@</span>function
  </h2>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      The thing most of us still use Sass for: functions. CSS is getting its
      own.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="main-gradient">Functions for your tokens</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      🧮 <span>Reusable </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">logic</span>
      <span>, defined once in CSS</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🌐 <span>Runs </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">at runtime</span>
      <span> - with live custom properties</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧳 <span>One less reason to </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">keep Sass around</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      A Sass function runs once, at build time, on static values. A CSS custom
      function runs in the browser, on the live value of a token - change the
      token, the result follows.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's the whole talk in one feature: the same idea, moved from the build
      to the browser.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h5 style="margin-block-end: 1em" class="center">
    <span class="main-gradient">A tint function, native</span> 🧮
  </h5>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="functions.css">
    <code style="font-size: 0.65em" is:raw class="language-scss">
    @function --tint(--color, --amount: 10%) {
        result: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color), white var(--amount));
    }

    article:hover {
        background: --tint(var(--accent), 15%);
    }</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Parameters are custom properties, --amount has a default (10%), and the
      result descriptor is the return value. Called like any function: --tint().
    </p>
    <p>
      Inside it's the color-mix() we saw in the colors section - now with a
      name, and reusable across the whole system.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide class="cover-internal-sub-section">
  <h2>Typed attr()</h2>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      attr() is old - but until now it only worked in content, as a string. Now
      it can return real types: colors, lengths, numbers.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="main-gradient">Tokens straight from HTML</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      🏷️ <span>Read an </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">HTML attribute</span>
      <span> as a typed value</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🎨 <span>A </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">color</span>
      <span>, a length, a number - not just a string</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧩 <span>Per-instance tweaks </span>
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">without inline styles</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Think CMS or design tool output: the content decides the accent of one
      card, the design system decides everything else.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h5 style="margin-block-end: 1em" class="center">
    <span class="main-gradient">attr() with a type</span> 🏷️
  </h5>
  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="card.css">
    <code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-scss">
    /* &lt;article data-accent="oklch(0.7 0.2 30)"&gt; */
    article {
        --accent: attr(data-accent type(&lt;color&gt;), oklch(0.7 0.15 250));
    }</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      type(&lt;color&gt;) tells the browser to parse the attribute as a color.
      The second argument is the fallback if the attribute is missing or
      invalid.
    </p>
    <p>
      It feeds the same --accent token as the card: border, hover tint,
      contrast-color - all follow, per instance.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide support>
  <h3 class="main-gradient center">About support</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th></th>
        <th>Chrome · Edge</th>
        <th>Firefox</th>
        <th>Safari</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr p-fragment p-effect="from left">
        <th>if()</th>
        <td data-status="ok">137+</td>
        <td data-status="no">-</td>
        <td data-status="no">-</td>
      </tr>
      <tr p-fragment p-effect="from left">
        <th><span style="font-family: Arial;">@</span>function</th>
        <td data-status="ok">139+</td>
        <td data-status="no">-</td>
        <td data-status="warn">TP</td>
      </tr>
      <tr p-fragment p-effect="from left">
        <th>typed attr()</th>
        <td data-status="ok">133+</td>
        <td data-status="ok">155+</td>
        <td data-status="warn">TP</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p p-fragment p-effect="zoom" class="center verdict">
    🌱 <span class="main-sub-gradient">Worth watching</span>, not betting on
  </p>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      if(): Chromium only. Firefox and Safari don't ship it yet (MDN: limited
      availability, experimental). If someone saw an @if or @when proposal:
      spec-level ideas, no browser ships them.
    </p>
    <p>
      @function: experimental. Safari has it in Technology Preview only - not in
      stable Safari.
    </p>
    <p>
      Typed attr(): the closest to Baseline - Chrome and Firefox shipped,
      Safari in Technology Preview.
    </p>
    <p>Transition into the summary map, then the honest limits.</p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<style lang="scss">
  [support] {
    table {
      margin: 0.6em auto 0;
      border-collapse: collapse;
      font-size: 0.6em;
    }

    th,
    td {
      padding: 0.45em 1.1em;
      text-align: center;
      border-block-end: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    }

    thead th {
      font-size: 0.75em;
      color: var(--ink-mute);
    }

    tbody th {
      text-align: start;
      font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
    }

    td {
      font-family: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, monospace);
      font-weight: 600;

      &[data-status="ok"] {
        color: light-dark(oklch(0.42 0.2 145), oklch(0.78 0.2 145));
      }
      &[data-status="warn"] {
        color: light-dark(oklch(0.55 0.2 75), oklch(0.82 0.18 75));
      }
      &[data-status="no"] {
        color: light-dark(oklch(0.5 0.22 25), oklch(0.72 0.22 25));
        opacity: 0.75;
      }
    }

    .verdict {
      font-size: 0.6em;
      margin-block: 1em 0;
    }
  }
</style>
